 ],
 "literal": "She indeed became like my mother, the chief lady of the festival, / so that the fresh pollen, clustered like green gram, would fall, / the soft branch with fragrant flowers bent down by the farmers / of the Kāñci tree, the lord of the town's cruelty / because she concealed, she comes indeed feeling ashamed.",
 "translation": "She has become like my mother to me, the chief lady of our domestic festival. Because she concealed the cruel unfaithfulness of the lord of the town—where farmers bend the soft, fragrant-flowered branches of the kāñci tree so that its fresh pollen, clustered like green gram, falls upon them—she now comes before us, filled with shame.",
 "modern": "விளையாட்டு அல்லது இல்லற விழாவின் தலைவியான என் தோழி (தலைவி), எனக்குத் தாயைப் போன்றவள் ஆகிவிட்டாள். பயறு போன்ற கொத்துக்களை உடைய புதிய மகரந்தம் தன் மேல் விழுவதற்காக, உழவர்கள் வளைத்த மணம் வீசும் பூக்களைக் கொண்ட மெல்லிய கிளைகளையுடைய காஞ்சி மரங்கள் நிறைந்த ஊரின் தலைவனுடைய கொடுமையை அவள் பிறர் அறியாமல் மறைத்தாள். அவ்வாறு மறைத்ததால், அவள் இப்போது நாணத்தோடு வருகிறாள்.",
 "explanation": "In this poem, the friend (tōḻi) speaks to the bystanders or to herself, praising the heroine's noble character. The colophon attributes this to the Pālai landscape (separation), though it contains rich Marutam (agricultural) imagery of the kāñci tree and farmers. The heroine is described as the 'chief lady of the festival' (domestic life), who has shown the maturity and protective nature of a mother ('yāy') by concealing the hero's unfaithful behavior ('koṭumai') to preserve his honor. The description of the farmers bending the soft branches of the kāñci tree to let the pollen fall on them serves as an implied metaphor (uḷḷurai): just as the farmers exploit the beautiful tree for their pleasure, the hero seeks pleasure elsewhere, yet the heroine patiently bears it. By hiding his faults, she feels a deep sense of shame ('nāṇiya') on his behalf, which shows her supreme dignity.",
